# S Corporation

An S Corporation is a small business corporation that combines pass-through taxation with corporate formalities and limited liability protection.

## Key Characteristics
- Pass-through taxation (no entity-level tax)
- Limited liability like C Corp
- Must file Form 2553 for S Election
- Max 100 shareholders
- One class of stock only
- Domestic corporation only
- Must pay reasonable compensation to employee-shareholders

## Tax Advantages
- Avoids double taxation
- Reduced self-employment tax (only on salary, not distributions)
- Distributions taxed as ordinary income (not qualified dividends)
- Potential estate planning benefits
- Tax-free health insurance for 2% shareholders

## Limitations
- Strict eligibility requirements
- Reasonable compensation requirement limits salary/distribution optimization
- Less flexibility in profit allocation than LLC
- Limited ability to raise capital
- Complex conversion if outgrowing S Corp status

## Formation Process
1. Form as C Corporation first
2. File Form 2553 (S Election) with IRS
3. Obtain shareholder consent
4. Elect appropriate tax year and accounting method

